# Permanent Responsive Luxury and Performance Rule

This project must remain visually luxurious, cinematic and consistent across desktop, tablet and real mobile devices.

Every future design, animation, layout or interaction change must be reviewed for all three device groups:

```text
Desktop: above 1024px
Tablet: 481px–1024px
Phone: 480px and below
```

Do not treat mobile and tablet as smaller copies of desktop.

Preserve the same visual idea, emotional impact, hierarchy and premium feeling, but adapt the implementation to suit each device’s screen size, input method and performance limits.

This rule applies to every future edit, even when the request only mentions desktop.

---

# 1. Every desktop change requires responsive adaptation

Whenever modifying a desktop section, component, animation or interaction:

1. Implement the requested desktop change.
2. Inspect how the same change behaves on tablet.
3. Inspect how the same change behaves on phone.
4. Create adapted tablet and phone behaviour where necessary.
5. Preserve the same design language and intended experience.
6. Validate that the adaptation does not damage existing mobile choreography.

Do not finish a task after checking desktop only.

Never assume desktop CSS, animation distances, blur values, video assets, hover states or scroll behaviour will work correctly on touch devices.

---

# 2. Preserve the luxury experience

Mobile optimisation must not mean removing the character of the website.

Preserve:

* Cinematic pacing
* Gold curtain and Venetian-strip effects
* Fog-rise transitions
* Layered menu panels
* Large editorial typography
* Slow storytelling sections
* Logo choreography
* Book Now button choreography
* Image stacking
* Soft shadows
* Gold highlights
* Elegant staggered movement
* Premium spacing
* Controlled text reveals
* Purposeful dwell time
* Parallax where safe
* Decorative particles where safe

Do not replace a signature animation with a generic fade unless explicitly requested.

Do not simplify the design into a standard mobile layout.

The goal is:

```text
Same luxury idea
Same emotional impact
Adapted implementation
Smooth real-device performance
```

---

# 3. Distinguish signature effects from ordinary effects

Before changing an animation, classify it.

## Signature effects

Examples:

* Homepage gold strips
* Homepage logo rise
* Book Now stretching animation
* Four-image fog storytelling sequence
* Layered three-colour mobile menu
* Major hero transitions
* Scroll-controlled editorial sections

Signature effects must preserve:

* Original order
* Original visual story
* Scroll relationship
* Timing hierarchy
* Stagger
* Dwell time
* Progression between phases

They may be optimised internally, but must not be converted into a generic one-time reveal.

## Ordinary effects

Examples:

* Standard card reveals
* Supporting paragraphs
* Small headings
* Decorative hover movement
* Minor icon animations
* Non-essential blur reveals

Ordinary effects may use cheaper phone implementations such as:

* Opacity
* Translate
* Short one-time transitions
* Reduced particle counts
* Reduced blur
* Disabled hover-only transforms

Do not apply ordinary mobile simplifications to signature effects.

---

# 4. Phone animation adaptation rules

At `max-width: 480px`:

## Scrolling

* Use native scrolling.
* Do not instantiate Lenis.
* Do not attach Lenis to the GSAP ticker.
* Do not create multiple scrolling engines.
* Preserve native momentum scrolling.
* Avoid rebuilding animations during browser-bar height changes.
* Ignore height-only viewport resize events.
* Rebuild only after meaningful width changes or orientation changes.

## ScrollTrigger

ScrollTrigger is allowed on phones when necessary for signature choreography.

Do not globally ban pinning.

Use this decision:

```text
Ordinary reveal:
Use IntersectionObserver or a short one-time reveal.

Signature scroll story:
Use a controlled ScrollTrigger timeline, sticky stage or shorter pin.
```

For phone pins:

* Keep the runway shorter than desktop.
* Avoid unnecessary 4–5 viewport hard pins.
* Use the minimum scroll distance that preserves pacing.
* Do not allow the sequence to finish before the user meaningfully scrolls through the section.
* Do not convert scroll-controlled signature sequences into automatic timelines.
* Do not complete the full sequence on section entry.

## Animation properties

Prefer:

* `transform`
* `translate3d`
* `scale`
* `opacity`
* CSS masks
* Gradient overlays
* Clip paths used carefully
* GSAP quick setters
* One requestAnimationFrame update path

Avoid:

* Animating width every frame when transform can produce the same result
* Large animated full-screen blur
* Repeated filter transitions
* Layout-affecting properties during scroll
* Updating many inactive elements on every scroll event
* Repeated `gsap.set()` loops for every element
* Permanent JavaScript animation loops

## Blur and fog

Do not globally remove blur.

For ordinary reveals:

* Use opacity and translate.
* Remove animated blur.

For signature fog effects:

* Keep a visible lightweight fog.
* Use approximately 2–3px blur where needed.
* Prefer a gradient, mask or pseudo-element.
* Preserve the soft image-covering transition.
* Do not turn the fog transition into a simple fade.

## Text animation

* Preserve large editorial text.
* Use word-level splitting on phones where character splitting is expensive.
* Preserve the intended stagger and hierarchy.
* Do not animate hundreds of characters individually unless performance is verified.

## Particles

* Keep decorative particles when they contribute to the design.
* Reduce the quantity on phones.
* Use transform and opacity only.
* Pause when outside the viewport.
* Pause when the document is hidden.
* Avoid permanent JavaScript loops.

---

# 5. Tablet adaptation rules

For `481px–1024px`:

Tablet must not automatically inherit either desktop or phone behaviour.

Evaluate:

* Portrait tablet
* Landscape tablet
* Touch input
* Medium GPU capability
* Reduced available width
* Browser chrome and safe areas

Tablet may keep more desktop animation detail than phone, but should use:

* Reduced scroll distances where needed
* Fewer simultaneous effects
* Smaller blur radii
* Responsive type sizes
* Adapted spacing
* Touch-safe controls
* No hover-dependent functionality
* Optimised media assets
* Stable viewport units

Do not alter tablet behaviour merely because a phone-specific fix exists.

Use explicit media queries and `gsap.matchMedia()` branches.

---

# 6. Protect existing signature choreography

Before modifying any animation file:

1. Inspect the existing timeline.
2. Identify each phase.
3. Record its progress range.
4. Identify the elements controlled by each phase.
5. Preserve the order unless the user explicitly asks to redesign it.

For scroll-controlled experiences, document the choreography internally using comments such as:

```ts
// 0.00–0.18: strips begin closing
// 0.18–0.52: logo rises
// 0.45–0.90: Book Now stretches
// 0.88–1.00: final landing and release
```

Do not compress all phases into one shared transition.

Do not use one global completed boolean for a multi-scene sequence.

Do not let one image transition complete the entire four-image story.

Do not remove future cards from the animation array.

Do not apply broad phone CSS that overrides GSAP-controlled opacity, transform, visibility or z-index.

---

# 7. Protect these specific experiences

## Four-image fog section

This section must always:

* Contain all four image transitions
* Reveal each image in order
* Allow each image to rise through fog
* Cover the previous visual
* Display the corresponding text
* Include dwell time
* Remain controlled by scroll
* Never stop after the first image

When changing this section, test every stage individually.

## Homepage hero

The homepage hero must preserve this progression:

1. Gold strips begin moving with scroll.
2. Strips continue progressively rather than finishing immediately.
3. The large logo rises back into view.
4. Logo letters retain their stagger.
5. The Book Now button stretches progressively.
6. Book Now letter spacing increases progressively.
7. Around 85–90% of hero progress, the button is near full width.
8. The hero releases only after the sequence is nearly complete.

Do not replace this with a one-time IntersectionObserver reveal.

## Mobile menu

The menu must keep three visibly separate layers:

```text
Dark gold: #8b6914
Light gold: #c9a96e
Cream: #ece8df
```

Opening order:

1. Dark gold
2. Light gold
3. Cream
4. Menu content
5. Link stagger

Closing order:

1. Content exits
2. Cream
3. Light gold
4. Dark gold

Do not collapse this into one fast panel.

Do not set all delays to zero.

Do not use a generic 200ms mobile drawer.

---

# 8. Media rules

Whenever adding or changing media:

## Video

* Provide a mobile source where possible.
* Use `<source media>` to prevent phones downloading the desktop asset.
* Use a poster.
* Pause outside the viewport.
* Pause when the document is hidden.
* Avoid repeated `play()` calls.
* Keep `muted` and `playsInline`.
* Respect reduced motion.
* Do not reduce desktop video quality.

## Images

* Use responsive image sizes.
* Use modern formats where possible.
* Avoid loading desktop-resolution images on phones.
* Provide correct `sizes`.
* Preserve art direction.
* Do not crop important luxury composition incorrectly.

---

# 9. Responsive layout rules

For every new section or change, verify:

* No horizontal overflow
* No clipped text
* No overlapping navigation
* No unsafe fixed-width containers
* No layout dependent on hover
* Correct safe-area padding
* Stable hero height
* Correct portrait and landscape behaviour
* Buttons remain tappable
* Typography remains editorial and intentional
* Spacing remains premium rather than cramped
* Fixed elements do not cover content
* Browser chrome changes do not cause jumps

Use:

* `svh` for stable minimum heights
* `dvh` only when resizing will not produce visual jumps
* `clamp()` for responsive typography and spacing
* Logical safe-area insets
* Component-specific media queries

Avoid global phone overrides that affect unrelated components.

---

# 10. CSS safety

When editing mobile CSS:

* Use selectors scoped to the component.
* Avoid broad selectors such as `*`, global `div` rules or generic animation classes.
* Do not add large groups of `!important` rules.
* Check whether CSS overrides GSAP inline styles.
* Consolidate conflicting custom properties.
* Do not create duplicate mobile definitions in several files.
* Keep phone rules under `max-width: 480px`.
* Keep tablet rules separate.
* Do not accidentally change desktop styles.

When possible, place responsive behaviour beside the component it controls.

---

# 11. Animation lifecycle and cleanup

Every animation must:

* Use a GSAP context or stored references.
* Clean up only its own triggers and observers.
* Never call:

```ts
ScrollTrigger.getAll().forEach((trigger) => trigger.kill());
```

* Avoid duplicate timelines after route changes.
* Avoid duplicate observers.
* Pause when hidden where appropriate.
* Remove temporary `will-change`.
* Avoid multiple resize listeners controlling the same animation.
* Avoid rebuilding from height-only mobile viewport changes.
* Avoid hydration-dependent layout jumps.

---

# 12. Git and regression protection

Before modifying a signature animation:

* Review the current Git diff.
* Preserve the last working choreography.
* Create a checkpoint when making a substantial animation change.
* Do not mix unrelated refactoring into animation work.
* Prefer small, reversible edits.
* Do not rewrite entire animation hooks unless necessary.

When a change affects mobile, compare against the last working mobile version rather than recreating the behaviour from memory.

